From Kim.Elmore at noaa.gov  Wed Sep 16 21:38:47 2009
From: Kim.Elmore at noaa.gov (Kim Elmore)
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2009 16:38:47 -0500
Subject: Table and order of rows and columns
Message-ID: <4AB15AE7.6030206@noaa.gov>

I have data frames of predictions and observation of three factors. Call 
these A, B, and c. I've built scoring functions that depend on table() 
returning a contingency table such that column n and row n are always 
for the same observed and predicted factor.  For example, I want table 
to return:

  a    b   c
a  I   J   K
b  L  M  N
c  O  P   Q

where I through Q are integers.

Table provides what I want *almost* all the time. But now I've hit a 
case where the rows and columns do not coincide. For example:

  a   b   c
b  L   M   N
c  O   P   Q
a   I   J   K

What can I do (or use) that will reliably match the order of the rows 
and columns?

Kim Elmore

--
Kim Elmore, Ph.D. (PP SEL/MEL/Glider, N5OP, 2nd Class Radiotelegraph, GROL)
"All weather is divided into three parts: Yes, No and Maybe. The greatest of these 
is maybe." -- The original Latin appears to be garbled.

From Kim.Elmore at noaa.gov  Wed Sep 16 22:28:56 2009
From: Kim.Elmore at noaa.gov (Kim Elmore)
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2009 17:28:56 -0500
Subject: Re: Table and order of rows and columns
In-Reply-To: <5B833E900330354F9FDA984D06F92428067B289F@ca-exchange.corp.pharsight.com>
References: <4AB15AE7.6030206@noaa.gov> <5B833E900330354F9FDA984D06F92428067B289F@ca-exchange.corp.pharsight.com>
Message-ID: <4AB166A8.3080902@noaa.gov>

Samer Mouksassi sent me in the right direction. Many thanks!

I've never seen this before, but playing with it, I got what I want.

Imagine a data frame, df, with "prediction" and "observation". If I
want table() to provide what I want, I need to do the following:

ordered(df$prediction) <- c("a","b","c")
ordered(df$observation) <- c("a","b","c")

Then table behaves as I wish. I was unaware that factors have an 
inherent order, but I'm wiser now.

Kim Elmore

