> > >
> > >
> > >
> > >       Dear S-plus users
> > >
> > >
> > >
> > >       On the historgram graph for an observed
> > >       distribution I want to overlay a normal density
> > >       distribution with the same mean and SD (and later in the analyses produce
> > >       graphs with several other theoretical
> > >       distributions).
> > >
> > >
> > > I use the following syntax, BUT the
> > >       normal distribution is displaced on the x-axis (and possibly also on the
> > >       y-axis).
> > >
> > >       The mean of the overlayed normal
> > >       distribution is farther to the right than the observed distribution. This
> > >       is probably because S-plus places the graph to fill the graph sheet
> > >       for the best presentation.
> > >
> > >
> > >
> > >       HOW do I manage to
> > >       have the overlay distribution placed using the same axis scaling than the
> > >       observed on both the x- and y-axis?
> > >
> > >
> > >
> > >       (the observed count variable is "mar")
> > >
> > >
> > >
> > >       hist(mar, nclass =
> > >       10,probability =T, xlab = "Number of Mar", ylab =
> > >       "Proportion of mar",main = "MAR",
> > > sub = "with normal distribution overlay",
> > >       density=15)
> > >
> > >
> > >
> > >       # Density for theoretic normal distribution
> > >       with same mean
> > > par(new = T) # for overlay at the
> > >       same graf
> > > plot(density(rnorm(50000,mean(mar),sqrt(var(mar)))),probability
> > >       =T,type="l", xlab = "", ylab = "",axes = F)
> > >
> > >
> > >
> > >
> > >
> > >       The
> > >       par(new=TRUE) tells the plotting system to forget about the
> > >       current
> > >
> > >       coordinate system (and to forget that anything is plotted
> > >       on the current
> > >
> > >       page).  Omit that line and
> > >       replace
> > >
> > >          plot(density(...),
> > >       ...)
> > >
> > >       with
> > >
> > >
> > >       lines(density(...))
> > >
> > >
> > >
> > >       The
> > >       complete script would be
> > >
> > >
> > >
> > >
> > >       hist(mar, nclass = 10,probability
> > >       =T, xlab = "Number of Mar", ylab = "Proportion of
> > >       mar",main = "MAR",
> > > sub = "with normal distribution overlay",
> > >       density=15)
> > >
> > >
> > >
> > >       # Density for theoretic normal
> > >       distribution with same mean
> > >  lines (density(rnorm(50000,mean(mar),sqrt(var(mar)))))
> > >
> > >
> > >
> > >
> > >       Note
> > >       that the comment is wrong -- that gives you the density
> > >       of
> > >
> > >       a
> > >       sample from a normal distribution.  To get the actual
> > >       density
> > >
> > >       use
> > >       the dnorm(x, mean, sd) function, as in
> > >
> > >
> > >
> > >        x<-seq(min(mar),max(mar),length=500)
> > >
> > >        lines(x,
> > >       dnorm(x,mean(mar),sqrt(var(mar))))
> > >
