From wdunlap at tibco.com  Sun Sep 20 19:50:29 2009
From: wdunlap at tibco.com (William Dunlap)
Date: Sun, 20 Sep 2009 12:50:29 -0700
Subject: Re: Problem with placement of 2nd graph as overlay on graph
In-Reply-To: <328EB91EB0104A6CBDC2D09A16F03E09@JansAthlon64>
References: <79144221DAB5416F82F5B5DA50782218@JansAthlon64> <77EB52C6DD32BA4D87471DCD70C8D70001CE5FF9@NA-PA-VBE03.na.tibco.com> <CE4C7D1B52D049E5AE4E04174D495754@JansAthlon64> <77EB52C6DD32BA4D87471DCD70C8D70001CE600A@NA-PA-VBE03.na.tibco.com> <328EB91EB0104A6CBDC2D09A16F03E09@JansAthlon64>
Message-ID: <77EB52C6DD32BA4D87471DCD70C8D70001CE6038@NA-PA-VBE03.na.tibco.com>

Use the dnbinom function.  Note the pattern: 
d<dist> is

the density function for the distribution 'dist'.  'p' 
gives

the distribution function, 'r' gives the random 
number

generator, and 'q' gives the quantile function (inverse 
of

the distribution function).  The distributions 
include

'binom', 'norm', 'lnorm', 'pois', 'nbinom', 'exp', 
'gamma', 'beta',

and 'f'.  I probably have left out 
some.

 

